---
// How this site works: a short, static tour of the world model for the
// curious, then how it was built. Every example on it is generated from the
// real world at build time, so it can't drift from what the home page
// actually does. The workshop on the island opens this page.
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import Reading from '../layouts/Reading.astro';
import Prose from '../components/blog/Prose.astro';
import { loadWorld } from '../world/load';
import { checkWorld } from '../world/schema';
import contract from '../renderers/types.ts?raw';

const world = await loadWorld();

// One place, as the renderers receive it, trimmed to one piece of scenery.
// Short arrays and objects stay on one line, like a person would write it.
const flat = (v: unknown): string =>
  Array.isArray(v)
    ? `[${v.map(flat).join(', ')}]`
    : v && typeof v === 'object'
      ? `{ ${Object.entries(v).map(([k, x]) => `${JSON.stringify(k)}: ${flat(x)}`).join(', ')} }`
      : JSON.stringify(v);
const compact = (v: unknown, indent = ''): string => {
  const one = flat(v);
  if (typeof v !== 'object' || v === null || one.length + indent.length <= 64) return one;
  const inner = indent + '  ';
  if (Array.isArray(v)) return `[\n${v.map((x) => inner + compact(x, inner)).join(',\n')}\n${indent}]`;
  return `{\n${Object.entries(v).map(([k, x]) => `${inner}${JSON.stringify(k)}: ${compact(x, inner)}`).join(',\n')}\n${indent}}`;
};
const taproom = world.places.find((p) => p.id === 'busy-beer')!;
const sample = compact({ ...taproom, scenery: taproom.scenery.slice(0, 1) });

// A real failure: cut the bottle off from every path and ask the checker.
const broken = structuredClone(world);
broken.routes = broken.routes.filter((r) => r.from !== 'contact' && r.to !== 'contact');
const failure = checkWorld(broken)
  .map((i) => `  • ${i.path.join('.')}: ${i.message}`)
  .join('\n');

const workshop = world.places.find((p) => p.id === 'workshop');

const themes = { light: 'github-light', dark: 'github-dark' } as const;
const views = [
  { id: 'island', name: 'Island', note: 'three.js, walk it in 3D' },
  { id: 'map', name: 'Map', note: 'canvas pixel art, top-down' },
  { id: 'text', name: 'Text', note: 'a parser and a prompt' },
  { id: 'list', name: 'List', note: 'plain HTML, no JavaScript' },
];
---
<Reading
  title="How this site works · Ambrose Vannier"
  description="The home page is one world described as data, drawn four ways: a 3D island, a pixel map, a text adventure and a list. And how it was built."
  current="none"
>
  <header class="col-head">
    <p class="col-kicker">Colophon</p>
    <h1 class="col-title">How this site works</h1>
    <p class="col-lede">The home page is one world described as data, and four different renderers draw it.</p>
    <p class="col-jump"><a href="#workshop">Skip to how it was built</a></p>
  </header>

  <ul class="col-views" role="list">
    {views.map((v) => (
      <li><a href={`/?view=${v.id}`}><span class="col-views__name">{v.name}</span><span class="col-views__note">{v.note}</span></a></li>
    ))}
  </ul>

  <Prose>
    <h2>The world is data</h2>
    <p>
      Everything on the island lives in one document: {world.places.length} places, {world.routes.length} routes between them,
      {' '}{world.lostWords.length} hidden words, the shape of the coast, and the {world.projects.length} projects and {world.posts.length} posts
      they stand for. It says what each place is and what you see when you get there. It never says how anything looks. Here's
      the taproom as every renderer receives it (trimmed to one piece of scenery):
    </p>
    <Code code={sample} lang="json" themes={themes} defaultColor={false} />
    <p>
      The island knows it's a <code>taproom</code>. The 3D view builds timber and string lights for that, the map draws a
      sprite, and the text adventure just reads you the description. Positions are in metres, and the coastline, hills and
      paths are a recipe that one shared geometry module turns into a height field, so the 3D island and the pixel map agree
      on where the beach is, and on how far out you can swim before the seabed drops away.
    </p>
    <p>You can explore the whole thing on <a href="/blueprint">the island's blueprint</a>. It's also published as <a href="/world.json">/world.json</a>, with a <a href="/world.schema.json">JSON Schema</a> generated from the same source.</p>
    <p>
      The island is only one way to lay all this out, so there's a second file with the island taken away:
      {' '}<a href="/pack.json">/pack.json</a> (and <a href="/pack.schema.json">its schema</a>). It has who I am, what I've made and
      written, what I care about, the site's colors and type, and the island's ideas without their coordinates: the lost words,
      the games, the wardrobe and the islanders. Whatever I build next, a racing game or something stranger, can start from it.
    </p>

    <h2>It can't be wrong quietly</h2>
    <p>
      The world is checked when the site builds. The schema catches typos and unknown fields. Then a second pass checks the rules
      that span the whole world: every project has exactly one place, every place can be reached from the plaza, nothing stands
      inside anything else, every hidden word is tucked into scenery that exists. If I cut the beach off from every path, the
      build stops with this:
    </p>
    <pre class="col-error"><code>{`The world doesn't hold together:\n${failure}`}</code></pre>

    <h2>Renderers are plug-ins</h2>
    <p>
      A renderer is anything that can mount into an empty element and later be paused or destroyed. The page around it owns
      everything they share: the switcher, the color wipe when you go into a place, the cards for the words you find, sound,
      and what to fall back to (no WebGL gets the map; reduced motion gets the list). Each renderer is loaded only when you pick
      it, so reading the list never downloads a 3D engine.
    </p>
    <details class="col-details">
      <summary>The whole contract</summary>
      <Code code={contract.trim()} lang="ts" themes={themes} defaultColor={false} />
    </details>

    <h2>One pair of pockets</h2>
    <p>
      What you've done is shared too: where you're standing, the words you've found, the posts you've caught off the pier. The
      rules are one pure function from what you did to what happens next, so they're tested without a browser. Walk to the
      library on the island, switch to the text adventure, and you're still at the library.
    </p>

    <h2>The games</h2>
    <p>
      Eight words English lost are hidden around the island, each where it belongs: <em>crapulous</em> behind the taproom,
      {' '}<em>wordhord</em> on the library's shelf, <em>curglaff</em> (the shock of cold water when you first plunge in) by the
      pier. They come from the museum of lost words in <a href="/work/etymon">Etymon</a>, and they're hidden in the data, so you
      can find them in any view. Find them all and night falls. And if you fish off the pier, whatever bites is something I wrote.
    </p>
    <p>
      There are three little games too, each one more activity in the data with a game id and a spot: skipping stones on the
      north beach along from the lighthouse, crab boop on the beach by the bottle, and a crate stack by the recycling depot. The 3D island
      and the pixel map play them in the same small card, the text adventure plays skipping stones in words, and your best at
      each is kept with everything else you've done, so every view knows it.
    </p>
    <p>
      In the middle of the plaza there's a portal. In the data it's one more activity, a kind and a spot; each view draws it in
      its own way and sends you on to the next one. Step through on the island and you come out of the same portal on the pixel
      map, then in the text adventure, then back on the island, standing exactly where you were.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every building has a room inside, and someone who lives there: a librarian, a barkeep, a lighthouse keeper. The rooms are
      data too, from what's on the walls to where everyone stands, so the island builds a little dollhouse, the map paints a
      pixel room and the text adventure lets you <em>ask Mabel about the river</em>. The islanders are made up. What they say
      about my work is only ever what its own page says, and they'll point you to it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Tied up at the end of the pier there's a speedboat. On the 3D island you can take it out and race a lap round the island,
      through a ring of gates out past the buoys, against the clock and a ghost of your best lap. The course is pure math, worked
      out from the same coastline as everything else, so it's tested like the rest of the world, and your best time is kept with
      your words and your catches. The map and the text adventure know about the boat too: they can tell you about it, and send
      you to the island to race it.
    </p>

    <h2 id="workshop">How it was built</h2>
    <p>
      There's a workshop on the island{workshop ? ', inside the railway loop' : ''}, with a monitor glowing in the window and a
      pinboard of notes on the wall. This is what's pinned up on it.
    </p>
    <h3>Checkpoints</h3>
    <p>
      The git history shows the rhythm. Long pieces of work are saved as they go in commits marked <em>checkpoint</em>, so
      nothing is lost if an idea goes sideways, and when a piece is finished it lands with a commit that says what it does.
    </p>

    <h3>A gate on every build</h3>
    <p>
      <code>npm run build</code> runs <code>astro check</code> for the types, then the tests, then the build itself, and a
      change isn't done until all three pass. The tests cover the parts that are pure logic: the world and its cross-checks,
      the store that remembers what you've done, the text adventure's parser and engine, the map's layout, and the scoring in
      the games. Whenever something new goes on the island, a test goes with it.
    </p>

    <h3>Looking, not just testing</h3>
    <p>
      Tests can't tell you a roof is the wrong color. So each piece is checked by eye, too: the site is built, served and
      photographed in a headless browser with <a href="https://playwright.dev">Playwright</a>, at desktop and phone
      sizes. Headless browsers often have no WebGL, so most of those checks go through the pixel map, which draws the same world
      from the same data. Then I look at the screenshots, and at the real thing.
    </p>

    <h2>Built with</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="https://astro.build">Astro</a> for the pages, <a href="https://threejs.org">three.js</a> for the island, the
      Canvas 2D API for the pixel map, <a href="https://zod.dev">zod</a> for the world's schema and
      {' '}<a href="https://vitest.dev">Vitest</a> for the tests. The contact form posts to a small Cloudflare Worker. It's set in
      Bricolage Grotesque and Newsreader, and hosted on Vercel. The blog is plain Markdown. The rest of the site has no
      framework on the client: the island, the map and the text adventure are TypeScript talking to the DOM.
    </p>
  </Prose>
</Reading>

<style>
  .col-head { margin-block-end: var(--space-6); }
  .col-kicker { font-size: var(--step--1); font-weight: 650; letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--muted); }
  .col-title {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-2);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: clamp(2.4rem, 1.6rem + 3.6vw, 4rem);
    font-weight: 700;
    line-height: 1.02;
    letter-spacing: -0.04em;
  }
  .col-lede { margin-block-start: var(--space-4); font-family: var(--font-read); font-size: var(--step-1); line-height: 1.45; color: var(--ink-2); max-width: 32ch; }

  .col-views { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: var(--space-3); margin: 0 0 var(--space-7); padding: 0; list-style: none; }
  .col-views a {
    display: grid;
    gap: 2px;
    min-height: 44px;
    padding: var(--space-4);
    border-radius: var(--r-md);
    background: var(--bg-sunken);
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: transform var(--dur-2) var(--ease-spring), box-shadow var(--dur-2);
  }
  .col-views a:hover { transform: translateY(-2px); box-shadow: var(--shadow-2); }
  .col-views__name { font-family: var(--font-display); font-weight: 700; font-size: var(--step-1); }
  .col-views__note { font-size: var(--step--1); color: var(--muted); }

  .col-error {
    white-space: pre-wrap;
    color: color-mix(in srgb, #c2361c 80%, var(--ink)) !important;
  }
  .col-jump { margin-block-start: var(--space-4); font-family: var(--font-ui); font-weight: 650; }
  .col-jump a { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; min-height: 44px; }

  .col-details { margin-block: var(--space-5); }
  .col-details summary { cursor: pointer; font-family: var(--font-ui); font-weight: 650; font-size: var(--step-0); color: var(--link); padding-block: 10px; }
</style>
